Implementation notes (practical and ethical)
- If you want the exact consultant phrase “I was praying for you by name this morning, {FirstName}, and felt led to write,” we should only use it if Pastor Don actually prays through the list (or prays aloud over the letters/names). I recommend one of these options:
  1. Pastor Don prays over the full names list (or the printed letters) once before signing and mailing — that makes “by name” defensible because the names were included in the prayer. This can be done in one focused session (e.g., 30–60 minutes), or
  2. Use the truthful variant above (“I was praying for you this morning, {FirstName},…”) which preserves warmth and honesty and avoids misleading donors.
- Use the “as I laid your letter before the Lord” phrase only if Don or the pastoral team will in fact lay the letters/partners before the Lord prior to mailing.
- Keep a simple log noting the date the pastor prayed (or the prayer session occurred) so the team can truthfully say the prayer was offered.

Brief moral rationale (from the Westminster Standards)
- The Westminster Confession and Catechisms uphold the moral law and the duty of truthfulness in Christian conduct. Claiming spiritual acts (like personal, by-name prayer) that were not in fact performed would misuse pastoral authority and would conflict with the biblical/moral duty to avoid falsehood. So we should not represent that Don prayed by-name unless he actually did; we should, however, be as warm and personal as we can while remaining truthful.

If you want, I can:
- Produce a version that literally uses the consultant’s exact phrasing on the assumption Don will pray the list (and add a short prayer schedule and tracking sheet for that), or
- Produce a print-ready layout with the merge fields placed for your mail house.
